#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "ef6bfc82-5993-514e-bbb4-6b3769bec0e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-752" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $e^{-x}$ centered about the point $x=3.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\frac{f'''(a)}{3!}(x-a)^3+\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=e^{-x}$ and $a=3.$\n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ f(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-{e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(3) & =-{e}^{-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-3}+(-{e}^{-3})(x-3)+\\frac{({e}^{-3})}{2!}(x-3)^{2}+\\frac{(-{e}^{-3})}{3!}(x-3)^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & ={e}^{-3}-{e}^{-3}(x-3)+\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{2}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-3}}{6}(x-3)^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e82fb0f9-372a-50ab-a6a5-c1dc8868a2d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-753" :question/problem "Use the first three terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x) = \\sqrt x$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{9.1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)\t= f(a) + f'(a)(x-a) + \\frac{f''(a)}{2!}(x-a)^2 +\\cdots,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(9) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{108})(x-9)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =3+\\frac{1}{6}(x-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(x-9)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x = 9.1$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{9.1}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9.1} & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(9.1-9)-\\frac{1}{216}(9.1-9)^{2} \\\\  & ≈3+\\frac{1}{6}(0.1)-\\frac{1}{216}(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =3.016\\,620,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places. \n\n**Note:** The exact value of $\\sqrt{9.1}$ is $3.016\\,621$ to six decimal places. So, our approximation is very good!"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a99f15f0-e5b3-51b6-805b-c2b619878df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\frac{8}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310581} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b7530c-83bd-5ac0-96c6-8760d5568370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{2}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310582} {:answer/id #uuid "6881f6f5-afce-58ae-8e97-da9ca59caa18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-\\frac{8}{3}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310583} {:answer/id #uuid "83d4e717-9e3d-5eb5-a262-3042d840a3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310584} {:answer/id #uuid "b42b4663-c671-57ef-8ee1-9fedf1c3d4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b42b4663-c671-57ef-8ee1-9fedf1c3d4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310585} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfded277-1151-5501-9e5d-a561e29acdde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "193d7529-18a9-52a2-adf9-54c39fa112ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-126840" :question/problem "Find the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)\\,\\,\\,\\, & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =0+(-2)(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{(0)}{2!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}+\\frac{(8)}{3!}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-2(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{4}{3}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d50619a-704a-5499-85b9-5d23ec3f9970" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310588} {:answer/id #uuid "87856dbf-902c-59be-bb7a-3a3550435861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310589} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a42b83-46a7-5c46-82f9-3c498e3885a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310590} {:answer/id #uuid "4d34fadd-e4f3-5e16-99f6-1bcabd9d503a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310591} {:answer/id #uuid "f500879d-cb34-5b3f-8d12-86e0c88b71f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d34fadd-e4f3-5e16-99f6-1bcabd9d503a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡3=0.141\\,12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310591} :answer-field/id #uuid "48568be9-3f4b-5e63-8a0b-a2303e2a924b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001d0357-fa46-5892-877e-9b6cc4712426" :question/math-academy-id "q-126874" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ Give your answer to $5$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =(0)+(-1)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(0)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(1)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=3$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sin}⁡3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3 & ≈-(3-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{6}(3-\\pi )^{3} \\\\  & =0.141\\,12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto five decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a6751f8-6b67-5612-afec-6ae58a831fdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.658" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310595} {:answer/id #uuid "017d588a-955d-593a-9b37-e3ecdef78382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.649" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310596} {:answer/id #uuid "414274ac-53e4-5f61-89fb-70e9c1fb6886" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.662" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310597} {:answer/id #uuid "02d32d88-c986-571f-9eb5-80727cf5f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.654" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310598} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc3d27c-8afd-530b-ab69-1b98a4104d3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.643" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02d32d88-c986-571f-9eb5-80727cf5f2b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡4≈-0.654" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310598} :answer-field/id #uuid "6758f2dd-c1cb-579e-9e06-00d5da985969" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5d85ddc-8eac-5e3b-93df-320d5221c983" :question/math-academy-id "q-126878" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=4$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡4 & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(4-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(4-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-0.654\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e27f2f6-d7d8-5808-8993-b6ceb35c4269" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e+2e(x+1)+\\frac{4e}{3}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310602} {:answer/id #uuid "be3d767d-585f-53c7-ab10-dea823b26d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{e}-\\frac{3}{e}(x+1)+\\frac{15}{2e}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310603} {:answer/id #uuid "12cec708-67aa-595f-843f-c52449c93cd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{e}+\\frac{2}{e}(x+1)+\\frac{16}{3e}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310604} {:answer/id #uuid "e70ff32a-6c24-5afa-a2c9-feda20647c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)-\\frac{16e}{3}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310605} {:answer/id #uuid "af4e4d25-4d14-515e-bcbf-693f035b427e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af4e4d25-4d14-515e-bcbf-693f035b427e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-{x}^{3}}=e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310606} :answer-field/id #uuid "e797b5a0-276d-5966-9af8-366ef5ea3761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f732745-f1b8-59e9-bc01-3d04798eb357" :question/math-academy-id "q-13911" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ about $x=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{3}}$ and $a=-1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ f(-1) & ={e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3(-1)^{2}{e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=-3e \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-3{x}^{2})\\cdot (-3{x}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{-{x}^{3}}+(-6x)\\cdot {e}^{-{x}^{3}}=(9{x}^{4}-6x){e}^{-{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(-1) & =(9\\cdot (-1)^{4}-6\\cdot (-1)){e}^{-(-1)^{3}}=15e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{3}} & =e+(-3e)(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2!}(x+1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =e-3e(x+1)+\\frac{15e}{2}(x+1)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a35d6ad3-66d5-54b9-ad61-b27832ac743c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310609} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b9baf-35f4-5daa-8f82-a3ebda5bbf9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{25}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310610} {:answer/id #uuid "b924906a-b743-5a33-bf80-b5bb23c8433e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310611} {:answer/id #uuid "0d19137a-016c-592c-ac06-92e62a799e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310612} {:answer/id #uuid "1e62c450-d266-553f-a4e4-c2147c6c79a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e62c450-d266-553f-a4e4-c2147c6c79a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{7}^{1/3}≈\\frac{23}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310613} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb597473-f05d-566a-988a-2db33154b28b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2001916-7ed2-504f-9a8e-d02fda09253a" :question/math-academy-id "q-19493" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{1/3}$ about $x=8$ to approximate the value of ${7}^{1/3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=8.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{1/3} \\\\ f(8) & ={8}^{1/3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-2/3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{-2/3}=\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{1/3} & =2+\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}(x-8)+⋯ \\\\  & =2+\\frac{x-8}{12}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ into the above to approximate ${7}^{1/3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{7}^{1/3} & ≈2+\\frac{7-8}{12} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0038b334-e6d3-5ea6-92f7-e9cc7fc417a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.095" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310616} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c075d1a-08c8-5a2f-9124-b9d5609b9985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f2c13f1-81f7-545f-882e-aa6e418d6c6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-347635" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\sqrt{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\sqrt{1.2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2} \\\\ f(1) & ={1}^{1/2}=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)+\\frac{(-1/4)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.2$ into the above to approximate $\\sqrt{1.2}.$ Since\n\n$$\n1.2-1=\\frac{1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1.2} & ≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5})-\\frac{1}{8}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{10}-\\frac{1}{200} \\\\  & =\\frac{219}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a3b73c8-441e-5595-af0a-40fc3c0044a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310619} :answer-field/id #uuid "76e2ac35-9b2d-5677-9538-808448878cfc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310618} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b364c5d0-46c3-5c92-834b-0849c46c5e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310621} :answer-field/id #uuid "771391b4-e95f-5fed-9c1d-e057532cc808" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310620} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d889759e-ad5c-52fb-8505-431f6294d5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310623} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e6a6a64-1c09-5353-bd0d-458d03cc46f5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81f76b8b-d322-5853-848e-fee21cd6de31" :question/math-academy-id "q-353904" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ about $x=e.$\n\n$x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-e)\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-e)^{2}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ and $a=e.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ f(e) & =e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)=e \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(e) & =\\frac{1}{e} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(e) & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =e+(2)(x-e)+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{e})}{2!}(x-e)^{2}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}})}{3!}(x-e)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =e+2(x-e)+\\frac{1}{2e}(x-e)^{2}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b5b4f0c-a8d4-5b33-8279-eaa99e44613d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310626} :answer-field/id #uuid "f396c657-75b7-585a-b01c-dda57dfb6953" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310625} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16752fcf-1e06-52cf-b22f-480f0d871686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310628} :answer-field/id #uuid "70011fbc-ce42-5c2c-9f80-987048adb492" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310627} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f2aa15e-2678-5509-85d1-d0e9b5d84fe5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310630} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc5fdec-6177-53d9-87fe-40b399c35de6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310629} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ba4e3d-1327-530e-8993-de1be5288167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310632} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c4be7e-dc44-57f7-90b0-9cdc7c2de488" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f93c24-fffd-543d-8271-aa03ba162e9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353905" :question/problem "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ about $x=1.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x=$ {{field-1}} $+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+\\,$\n{{field-4}}\n$(x-1)^{3}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =1+1=2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{‴}(1) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x & =1+2(x-1)+\\frac{-1}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{3!}(x-1)^{3}⋯ \\\\  & =1+2(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)^{3}⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3146eb6-b4ef-554d-83c1-351a3fb15651" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Terms of the Taylor Series of a Function About a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d424f9-994f-5e5f-8a16-d0e21575bdd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310635} {:answer/id #uuid "bb6ac4dc-fc04-54e5-9bb8-5e2b2fc3524c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb6ac4dc-fc04-54e5-9bb8-5e2b2fc3524c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310636} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e91e638-9a0f-5257-bdcb-466b2c7df9c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e6ce05-f3ac-5192-a716-ebfbf206cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1e6ce05-f3ac-5192-a716-ebfbf206cc8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310638} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c01ef3-bb1d-5089-893f-524c6e531279" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310637} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bf25ab4-42e5-5454-88ac-5de4f783d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bf25ab4-42e5-5454-88ac-5de4f783d11f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310640} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5960653-e1a0-5b43-a7a5-33a593a2293f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98ed4bd4-cd51-5bf7-857e-4a26fd6e986f" :question/math-academy-id "q-353917" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ centered about the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+$\n{{field-2}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n${(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}\\,+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ f\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-cos\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-1. \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-1+0\\,(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{2!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}+\\frac{0}{3!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{3}+\\frac{(-1)}{4!}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}\\,{(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{4}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186310641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a239976c-cf11-5c26-b250-2c03b3fb1ec3" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Value of a Function at a  Point Using Its Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1046fd6c-acba-57b2-ad93-a7da4843749d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.505" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310643} {:answer/id #uuid "78ebe08a-e948-50ad-bb83-519eaa6130d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310644} {:answer/id #uuid "416b5de9-6551-5797-a7e0-fa0241d16519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.492" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310645} {:answer/id #uuid "f86c4e2c-821b-5f1b-8966-2050231d4f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.601" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310646} {:answer/id #uuid "b391f9db-83f6-5f8e-896f-5263730b5624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.486" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78ebe08a-e948-50ad-bb83-519eaa6130d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1)≈-0.542" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310644} :answer-field/id #uuid "32339ada-d762-57ea-a7f2-d25137a77429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70e48cac-b202-5690-92a9-bc78cef33119" :question/math-academy-id "q-83201" :question/problem "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of  $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ Give your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi =0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =-1+\\frac{1}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{4!}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(x-\\pi )^{4}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +1$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi +1) & ≈-1+\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{24}(\\pi +1-\\pi )^{4} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{24} \\\\  & =-0.542,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places."}]}
